# Vedanta Kitchen Research & Evidence Standard

This rule exists so the SOP system is built from authoritative evidence and real Vedanta operating data, not generic AI text or random internet advice.

## 1. Source hierarchy
When researching or drafting safety, hygiene, allergen, equipment, cleaning or operational content, use sources in this order unless the context requires otherwise:

### Tier 1 — law / regulator / competent authority
For UK food safety and workplace safety, prefer:
- Food Standards Agency (FSA): https://www.food.gov.uk/
- FSA Safer Food Better Business (SFBB): https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/safer-food-better-business-sfbb
- FSA MyHACCP where appropriate: https://myhaccp.food.gov.uk/
- Health and Safety Executive (HSE) catering guidance: https://www.hse.gov.uk/catering/
- HSE COSHH: https://www.hse.gov.uk/coshh/
- GOV.UK / legislation where a legal requirement must be confirmed.

Do not rely on blogs or search-result summaries for statutory limits.

### Tier 2 — exact manufacturer documentation
For any piece of equipment, use the exact model's current manufacturer manual, safety instructions, cleaning instructions and service requirements.
Examples of manufacturer source libraries relevant to Vedanta include:
- RATIONAL documents: https://www.rational-online.com/en_gb/customercare/downloads/documents/
- Falcon Foodservice user manuals: https://www.falconfoodservice.com/info-centre/literature/user-manuals
- Falcon install/service manuals: https://www.falconfoodservice.com/info-centre/literature/install-manuals
- MEIKO downloads/manuals: https://www.meiko.com/en-gb/downloads-media/downloads

Never substitute a similar model's instructions without clearly flagging it as unverified.

### Tier 3 — site-approved professional controls
Use Vedanta's own approved documents where they are stricter or more operationally specific and do not conflict with law/manufacturer requirements:
- site risk assessments
- HACCP/SFBB records
- COSHH assessments
- SDS for the exact chemical product
- EHO-agreed controls
- approved SOPs
- maintenance/service records
- fire-safety procedures
- approved allergen policy

### Tier 4 — measured Vedanta live-kitchen evidence
For productivity, workflow, staffing, task duration, yield, waste, queueing and practical operating sequence, prefer measured site data over generic internet benchmarks.

### Tier 5 — reputable professional references
Use recognised professional hospitality/catering bodies, technical guidance and peer-reviewed or industry evidence only when Tiers 1–4 do not answer the question.

### Never treat as authoritative
- random blogs
- marketing copy
- social-media posts
- anonymous forum claims
- AI-generated text without source traceability
- generic timing benchmarks presented as mandatory standards

## 2. Conflict resolution
When sources conflict, use this logic:
1. applicable law/regulator requirement
2. exact manufacturer safety/instruction requirement
3. documented site risk assessment / approved food-safety system
4. approved Vedanta SOP
5. measured live-kitchen practice
6. professional reference / benchmark

For non-safety productivity questions, measured Vedanta data should normally outrank generic benchmarks.

Never use live practice to override a legal, food-safety or manufacturer safety requirement.

## 3. Evidence must be stored, not just read
Design the SOP data model so important content can be traced to evidence.

At minimum, an evidence/source record should be capable of storing:
- source_id
- source_type: regulator / law / manufacturer / SDS / risk_assessment / Vedanta_SOP / service_record / training / observation / other
- publisher / organisation
- title
- URL or file reference
- jurisdiction
- manufacturer
- equipment model
- document/version/revision
- publication date if known
- retrieved/attached date
- exact page/section/paragraph reference where practical
- notes
- verified_by
- verified_at
- review_due_at
- status: unverified / verified / superseded
- file hash/version identifier where practical

Create link records so one SOP, one SOP step, one critical limit, one equipment item or one training item can reference multiple evidence sources.

## 4. Safety-critical values require provenance
Examples:
- fridge limits
- hot holding limits
- blast chilling/cooling criteria
- cooking/reheating validation
- chemical dilution/contact time
- fryer oil-fill requirement
- equipment operating temperature range
- cleaning-cycle chemicals
- maintenance intervals

Do not put these values into UI components as unexplained constants.
Store them as configuration/domain data with:
- value
- unit
- scope
- source/evidence
- effective date
- review date
- approval

Changing a safety-critical value must create an audit event/version.

## 5. Current UK baseline examples — verify before implementation
These examples show how evidence should work; they are not permission to hard-code values without a linked source.

- FSA guidance states cold food must be kept at 8°C or below in England, Wales and Northern Ireland, and recommends setting fridges at 5°C or below to allow for fluctuations.
- FSA SFBB provides structured chilling/cooling methods and corrective-action expectations; blast chilling is recognised as a method designed to cool hot food quickly and safely.
- FSA allergen guidance requires food businesses to provide allergen information and manage allergens effectively; staff allergen training is required.
- HSE catering guidance identifies slips/trips, manual handling, contact with hot surfaces/harmful substances, dermatitis and knife cuts as major catering risks.
- HSE knife guidance requires task-suitable knives, stable cutting surfaces, training, secure storage and safe carrying/handling practices.
- HSE advises that fryer emptying/cleaning should follow manufacturer instructions.
- HSE COSHH guidance for catering covers exposure to cleaning products, wet work, fumes, ventilation, PPE and safe storage of cleaning chemicals.

Use the current regulator page/manual itself as the source in the product, not this summary.

## 6. Exact equipment rule
Before producing an operational SOP for a machine, collect:
- manufacturer
- exact model
- serial number if available
- rating-plate photo
- front photo
- control-panel photo
- installed location
- power/fuel type where relevant
- exact manual
- cleaning manual/section
- service/maintenance instructions
- local isolator/emergency controls where appropriate
- current condition / known faults
- staff currently competent to use it

If exact identity is missing, create only an equipment placeholder and a data-collection task. Do not publish operating instructions.

## 7. Manufacturer manual ingestion workflow
When a manual is supplied or found:
1. verify exact manufacturer + model match
2. store document metadata
3. identify safety warnings
4. identify permitted user operations
5. identify prohibited user operations / engineer-only work
6. identify start-up, normal operation, shutdown and cleaning instructions
7. identify maintenance/service intervals
8. capture page/section citations
9. draft Vedanta quick SOP
10. competent-person/site review
11. practical validation on the real machine
12. approve and version

AI may assist extraction and drafting but must never auto-approve.

