## 9. Build a benchmark engine from Vedanta data, not guesses
For prep/cleaning/task-time guidance:
- initial external or chef estimates must be labelled `provisional`
- do not present a single guessed time as a performance requirement
- record actual observations
- group by task, quantity, role, equipment, cover band and relevant complexity
- calculate median and useful percentile/range after enough observations
- retain sample count
- exclude or flag observations affected by equipment faults, incidents or exceptional interruptions
- never generate competitive speed leaderboards
- never penalise staff automatically for taking longer
- safety and quality always outrank speed

Recommended benchmark fields:
- task_type
- quantity_band
- unit
- role_band
- equipment_context
- cover_band
- sample_count
- median_duration
- p25/p75 or robust range
- last_calculated_at
- confidence/status
- approved_for_planning

## 10. Context-aware operational intelligence
When the app later estimates labour/task planning, consider:
- covers
- menu complexity
- dietary/allergen modifications
- batch size
- equipment availability
- staff competency
- prep already completed
- delivery timing
- cleaning/deep-clean workload
- maintenance downtime

Do not estimate from covers alone.

## 11. SOP lifecycle
Use a controlled lifecycle:
DRAFT → TECHNICAL REVIEW → FOOD-SAFETY/SAFETY REVIEW WHERE NEEDED → SITE VALIDATION → APPROVED → PUBLISHED → SUPERSEDED/RETIRED

An approved version must be immutable. Editing creates a new draft revision.

Operational records must retain the SOP revision used at the time.

## 12. Approval responsibility
The system should support separate approval dimensions when relevant:
- operational owner (e.g. Head Chef)
- food safety / allergen reviewer
- H&S / competent person
- equipment competent person / engineer if required

Not every SOP needs all approvers, but high-risk procedures may.

## 13. Chemicals / COSHH
For cleaning chemicals, collect the exact product and supplier documentation:
- product name
- supplier/manufacturer
- SDS
- COSHH assessment
- intended use
- dilution
- contact time
- PPE
- incompatibilities
- first aid/spill response
- storage
- expiry/review

Never infer dilution/contact time from another brand's product.

## 14. Allergens
Allergen logic must be evidence-backed and recipe/ingredient driven.

Support the UK 14 regulated allergens, but also allow site-specific dietary/intolerance notes outside those 14.
Do not convert a preference into an allergy or an allergy into a preference.
Do not infer absence of an allergen solely because a previous menu did not list it.
Supplier/ingredient changes must be able to trigger allergen re-verification.

## 15. Research logging
When Cursor or an AI-assisted workflow researches content, capture a research note containing:
- question asked
- sources consulted
- date
- relevant excerpt/summary
- applicability to Vedanta
- unresolved assumptions
- who must verify

This can live as structured data later; during development, maintain it in docs/ or test fixtures where appropriate.

## 16. Product principle
The final system should be able to answer not just:
`What is the SOP?`

but also:
- Why is this requirement here?
- Which source/manual supports it?
- Which equipment model does it apply to?
- Who approved it?
- Which version was used on this shift?
- What happened when it failed?
- What does our own kitchen data show in practice?

That evidence trail is a core product feature, not optional documentation.